Chapter 4 — What Is the Non Data-Based Approach Chapter 4 WHAT IS THE NON DATA-BASED APPROACH Spring 1988. A discovery made not in a laboratory — not through research — but through the honest observation of what was actually happening in the encounter between accumulated knowledge and fresh reality. First transmitted to Eric — a citizen from San Diego — who found it valuable. No publication. No institution. No framework. Simply the most honest observation available about what happens when a consciousness field approaches any situation without the look-up table of accumulated precedent determining what should be seen before what is actually there has been perceived. The non data-based approach is the capacity to approach any situation — any problem — any relationship — any encounter — without being attracted by the outcome — without the conglomerate of desire for a specific result pulling the perception toward what is wanted rather than what is actually there. This is not objectivity. Not detachment. Not emotional distance. It is the most engaged — most attentive — most genuinely present — perception available to any consciousness field in any clock cycle now. It was being worked on in a Robotics Lab near Paris — Massy — in late 1986 — as the integration of Control — Vision — Planning into one coherent system. Without knowing that was what it was. Control is the draining system responding in each clock cycle. Vision is the non data-based perception of what is actually there. Planning is the forward-looking conscientious awareness of consequence. The integration challenge of 1986 was the Physics of Relationships — in a robotics lab — 40 years before the plain language arrived.

